From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: maxffarrell <72176209+maxffarrell@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 15:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] soundwire: qcom: re-enumerate the Dell XPS 13 9345's WSA
 buses on runtime resume

The Dell XPS 13 9345 has a woofer and a tweeter WSA8845 per side, each
pair on its own v2.0.0 WSA SoundWire bus. After a bus clash
("qcom_swrm_irq_handler: SWR bus clsh detected"), that bus's two
amplifiers can come back from clock stop unattached and stay that way
across every later resume, so one side of the laptop is silent until a
reboot. It was seen on both buses (6ab0000 and 6b10000) over two days.

Take the path 2438 gave the v3.1.0 controllers on this board too: mark
clock stop as unsupported, so runtime suspend skips the clock stop and
runtime resume soft-resets the controller and enumerates again, as at
probe.

Tested as an out-of-tree module, otherwise identical to the shipped
7.3-75 soundwire-qcom (the unmodified rebuild's .text matches it byte
for byte). In 10 traced idle/play cycles every amplifier attached again
50 to 300 ms after resume. In 15 more, with a tone per channel recorded
on the internal microphones, no channel stayed silent into the next
cycle, including after a real bus clash with parity errors during the
run; before, a dropped pair stayed silent until a reboot. The cost is
the reset itself: in 4 of those 15 cycles one channel's first 0.4 s
tone came out 20 to 30 dB low, so the start of a sound after a few
seconds of silence can be clipped on one side.
---
 drivers/soundwire/qcom.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
--- a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
@@ -669,8 +669,15 @@
 		 * from a clock stop unattached, or attached but returning
 		 * garbage for every register read. Reset the controller and
 		 * re-enumerate on runtime resume instead, as at probe.
+		 *
+		 * The Dell XPS 13 9345's two v2.0.0 WSA buses do the same
+		 * after a bus clash: that bus's woofer and tweeter stay
+		 * unattached across every later clock stop exit, silencing
+		 * one side until a reboot.
 		 */
-		ctrl->clock_stop_not_supported = ctrl->version >= SWRM_VERSION_3_1_0;
+		ctrl->clock_stop_not_supported =
+			ctrl->version >= SWRM_VERSION_3_1_0 ||
+			of_machine_is_compatible("dell,xps13-9345");
 		/* Now compare with entries */
 		list_for_each_entry_safe(slave, _s, &bus->slaves, node) {
 			if (sdw_compare_devid(slave, id) == 0) {
